{
  "example": "{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_path_method`\n\nFor the `ReviewNodeId` enum above, a requirement with the dotted name `a.b.c` is\nrepresented by `ReviewNodeId::Requirement(vec![\"a\".into(), \"b\".into(), \"c\".into()])`.\nIts `node_path` implementation maps that identity to\n`[\"requirement\", \"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Example.** `requirements.sdk.graph.hierarchical-paths` is a concrete case. A requirement named `a.b.c` is identified as `Requirement(vec![\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"])`. Its `node_path` maps that identity to `[\"requirement\",\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"]`. It cites `requirements.sdk.graph.node_path_method`, which asks for a stable, deterministic mapping.\n\n**Test.** `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/contracts.rs#fn[hierarchical_requirement_identity_maps_each_component_to_a_path_segment]` is the only `{test}` reference to the example. It does the following:\n- It builds the identity `Requirement(vec![\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"])`.\n- It maps the identity to a `NodePath` and asserts `.segments()` equals `[\"requirement\",\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"]`. This checks the expected output the example states, including the `requirement` prefix and one segment per dotted component.\n- It asserts that mapping the same identity twice gives equal paths. This covers the determinism the cited `node_path_method` requires.\n\n**Why a local enum is acceptable.** The test uses a local enum and closure instead of a real `ReviewNodeId`. A search of `crates` found no `ReviewNodeId` outside the book chapter `md/requirements/sdk/graph.md`, where it is an illustrative domain. No production `ReviewNodeId` or `node_path` implementation exists to test, so the test models the example's own illustrative domain. The test comment says so.\n\n**Limit of the test.** The test does not implement the `Domain` trait or call `Domain::node_path`, so it checks only `NodePath::new` and `segments()` plus the mapping the example describes. Distinctness of paths is part of `node_path_method`, but this example does not state it, so it is not an obligation here. The example's stated input and output are asserted exactly, and I found no gap against the example's text.",
